<!-- tick — a DEVICE LIFECYCLE HOOK: the block it opens runs once per //nalu//, the changeset wave:

         <<~ tick>>
           <<~ lele "lar:///a.b.c/x">>
         <<~/tick>>

     The nalu sits BELOW the grammar — the engine delivers the wave, and no operator emits one. tick
     names the grammar-layer hook INTO that wave, so a block under it sees one coherent delivered
     state per crest rather than each scattered write.

     THE PROPER NAME AND THE FULL LAW STAND DEFERRED, pending Visual Verse tick-node confirmation;
     the hook holds its grammar and its place in the family meanwhile.

     THE PAGE STAYS CLEAR. A lifecycle hook governs execution and never layout, so the definition
     hands the compile layer a data annotation naming the wave and renders nothing. -->

\procedure ~tick()
<span class="lar-tick" data-hook="nalu" hidden="true"/>
\end
